// A diagnostic against an imported module names the file that module came
// from, whichever module was parsed last.
//
// This is a regression scenario. lexInject re-used a popped Lexer block for
// the next injected source, and an IR node holds the Lexer that was current
// when it was built and reads its url when a diagnostic is reported -- long
// after parsing. So the second import overwrote the url of the block every
// node of the first import still pointed at, and moduleprov's error was
// reported against moduleprov2.cone while echoing moduleprov's source line.
// The file url was the only part that was wrong, which is what made it easy to
// read past. It also named conec's output files after the wrong source.
//
// The assertion is the annotation, which lives in moduleprov.cone because an
// annotation matches only a diagnostic reported against its own file. Nothing
// is annotated here: this file is error-free, and its whole job is to import
// the two modules in that order.
//
// Type-check stage only. Both modules parse and resolve cleanly, so the
// diagnostic is reached; a name-resolution error anywhere would return before
// type checking and the file would assert nothing.

import moduleprov::*
import moduleprov2::*

fn caller() i64 {
  untroubled(1i64)
}
